# whitelist_hashes.txt (EXAMPLE; copy to whitelist_hashes.txt)
#
# Verified false positives: files a human has looked at and ruled legal.
#
# One eD2k hash (32 hex chars) per line. A ';' starts an inline comment — use it
# to record WHY the entry is here, since that is the only record of the decision.
#
# SCOPE: an entry here overrides EVERY filter layer, not just the hash lists.
#   That is deliberate. The false positives that occur in practice are TERM
#   matches, not hash matches: song titles that happen to contain a marker word,
#   historical medical texts whose title contains one. An override that left the
#   term layers running did nothing for the only class of mistake that happens.
#
# The override is per-FILE, not per-name: a different file sharing the same name
# is still filtered normally.
#
# Hot-reloaded within ~30 s of an mtime change. Copy with `cp` and NOT `cp -p`,
# or the mtime does not change and nothing reloads.
#
# An EMPTY file is applied as written — clearing the list is how an override gets
# retracted. A failed READ is different and keeps the current list, so a
# permissions slip cannot silently re-block everything.
#
# 0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef ; "<Band> - <Song>.mp3" — real song title
